WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.583
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Cat-Out has decided

00:00:01.583 --> 00:00:04.200
to set up their packaging
subscriber on Cloud Functions.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.610
Stay tuned to find out how.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.810
So far in the series, we
learned about Cloud Pub/Sub

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.870
and how it works using Cat-Out's
Asynchronous Order Processing

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:18.740
Service.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.960
We saw how Pub/Sub helps
manage the interactions

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.690
between the order, packaging,
shipping, and notification

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:24.710
services.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.440
In the sixth episode, we
learned that Cloud Pub/Sub

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.650
supports both push and
pull message delivery.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:31.640
Quick recap.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:35.150
You use pull if you don't have
public HTTPS endpoints, which

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:38.450
means your service is private
or firewalls are involved.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.640
Pull is also more efficient
and minimizes latency.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.480
So if events are generated
at a really high rate,

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.970
like hundreds or thousands
of events per second,

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:48.930
then pull is the way to go.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:52.670
Also, push delivery requires the
client endpoint is a publicly

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.920
accessible HTTPS server.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.920
Luckily, this is easy to
set up with Cloud Functions.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.200
Today, let's dive
a bit deeper into

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.240
push subscribers for Cat-Out.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.250
Setting up Pub/Sub
to send messages

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.920
to a client using push
delivery is extremely easy.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.820
It requires no special
client libraries, just

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.400
a standard HTTPS
endpoint routable

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.270
by DNS and a web server to
respond to these requests.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.290
It's also a lightweight
way of programming,

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.290
particularly with
web server frameworks

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.290
such as Cloud Functions,
Cloud Run, and App Engine.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.870
You simply write the server
code and let the framework

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:28.860
take care of the communication.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.950
Of course, you can choose
to host this application

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.250
on a virtual machine
anywhere you like.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.930
It's usually more effort,
but if that's something

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.490
you need to do.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:39.410
But our friends at
Cat-Out are all in

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:40.830
for the lightweight programming.

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.460
So they decided to use Cloud
Functions for the packaging

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.440
subscriber that
receives messages

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.230
from the order service.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:50.780
That Pub/Sub push request coming
to the packaging subscriber

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.970
would be HTTPS post.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:56.680
That looks like this, where the
data field is base64 encoded,

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.270
and the subscription is defined.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.520
Once processed, the
packaging subscriber

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.820
sends a success response, like
200, 201, 202, 204, and 102

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.580
are interpreted as
message acknowledgments

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.620
by the Cloud Pub/Sub system.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.080
Pub/Sub does not send a
negative acknowledgment.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.770
So if the packaging webhook
does not return a success code

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:21.200
within the default
acknowledgment deadline,

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.960
then Pub/Sub retries
delivery until the message

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.860
expires after the subscription's
message retention period.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.680
Oh, and the cool part,
the rate of push delivery

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.090
is automatically adjusted
to maximize delivery rate

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.740
while not overwhelming
the push endpoint.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:40.070
This is accomplished using
a slow-start algorithm.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.490
Let's see how this works.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:45.470
The system starts by sending
a single message at a time.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:48.920
With each successful delivery,
the number of messages sent

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.030
is doubled.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:53.330
And the rate continues to
double until there is a delivery

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:57.500
failure or the system reaches
a quota or resource limits.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.510
For each delivery
failure, the number

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:01.610
of concurrent requests
to the endpoint

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:05.520
halves until a minimum of
one request is reached.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.000
So this algorithm
assumes that there

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.520
are enough published
messages in the queue

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.110
to sustain this throughput.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.090
Ultimately, the
push delivery rate

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.030
is constrained by the rate at
which messages are published.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:20.270
We understand the basics of
how a message is structured

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.250
and the slow-start
algorithm works.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.180
But we did not discuss the most
important piece of the puzzle--

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.300
how does Cat-Out authenticate
and authorize Pub/Sub

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.030
to make a call to the
subscriber endpoint?

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.510
There are a few options.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:34.152
Let's see.

00:03:34.152 --> 00:03:36.110
They could just set up
the packaging subscriber

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:39.560
with no authentication, which
is not a really good option

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:40.760
for a private service.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.250
So the real first option
for authentication

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:45.950
is that the publisher sends
a custom authorization

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:47.990
token as a part of the message.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:50.880
The subscriber then
authenticates that token.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.640
The second
authentication option is

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:54.470
to configure the
push subscription

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:56.900
to include a signed
OpenID Connect

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.920
token for a GCP service account
in the authorization header.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.870
And the push endpoint
can use this token

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:06.290
to validate that the request is
issued on behalf of the service

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.330
account associated
with the subscription

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:10.560
and make an
authorization decision.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.350
We know that Cat-Out
has chosen to set up

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.780
Cloud Function as the
packaging subscriber.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.410
With Cloud Function,
the authentication

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.260
and authorization
is even easier,

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:21.350
because it uses the
standard IAM permissions.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.180
Your code just
handles the message

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.540
and attributes while Function
manages the request, parsing,

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:28.670
and responding to Pub/Sub

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:31.280
To find out how simple
it is, let's set this up

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.030
and see for ourselves.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:36.350
So let's create a Pub/Sub
topic and then create a Cloud

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:38.510
Function in the same
project by clicking

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:42.020
on the Trigger Cloud Function
button right from the topic

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:42.890
itself.

00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:46.460
It autopopulates the trigger
as Cloud Pub/Sub, the topic,

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:47.840
and the function name.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.600
Once the function is
created, we go to our topic

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.050
and publish a message.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.320
Now it is time to check
if the message was

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:57.160
received by our Cloud
Function subscriber.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.020
We navigate to Logs,
filter on Cloud Function,

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.220
and see that our
message is received.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.330
Now, you may ask, what if
Cat-Out's packaging subscriber

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:07.940
has to go in maintenance
mode for some time?

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.810
In temporary
situations like that,

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.220
it is recommended to change
the subscription to pull.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:15.470
And when the service is
ready to take requests,

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:19.310
just set the URL to a
valid endpoint again.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:22.430
If Cat-Out decides to change
the packaging subscriber

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:24.680
to a different
service altogether,

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:27.920
then they would have to
permanently stop delivery

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.480
to the old endpoint.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:32.120
In such cases, it is
recommended to just delete

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.220
the subscription,
create a new one,

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.150
and add the new
subscriber to it.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:36.650
All right.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:40.550
So today we learned that push
subscriber is a simple HTTPS

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:41.330
endpoint.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.730
We learned the different
authentication authorization

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:46.910
mechanisms and saw how
easy it is to set up a push

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:49.310
subscriber on Cloud Functions.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:50.930
Stay tuned for the
next episode, where

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:53.120
we will help Cat-Out
set up a subscriber

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.100
endpoint on Cloud Run.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.290
Until then, give us
a like, subscribe,

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:01.030
and let us know what you want
to learn about Cloud Pub/Sub.

